    note: 'Primary alcohols (–OH at end of chain) oxidise to aldehydes then acids. Secondary alcohols (–OH in middle) oxidise only to ketones — they stop there. This difference is why some alcohols become valuable solvents (acetone from isopropanol) while others become acidic.'
  },
  acidbase: {
    title: 'Acid–Base neutralisation',
    tags: [{ label: 'Neutralisation', col: '#0C447C', bg: '#E6F1FB' }, { label: 'Ionic', col: '#085041', bg: '#E1F5EE' }],
    equation: 'HCl  +  NaOH  →  NaCl  +  H₂O\nCH₃COOH  +  NaHCO₃  →  CH₃COO⁻Na⁺  +  H₂O  +  CO₂',
    subtitle: '(acid + base → salt + water — the most fundamental reaction type)',
    what: 'An acid donates a proton (H⁺); a base accepts it. The products are a salt plus water. Strong acid + strong base (HCl + NaOH): complete neutralisation, pH 7 at stoichiometric ratio. Weak acid + base (vinegar + baking soda): CO₂ gas is produced, pH depends on the ratio.',
    real: 'Antacids (NaHCO₃, CaCO₃, Mg(OH)₂) neutralise excess stomach acid (HCl, pH ~1.5). Baking: acid in buttermilk + baking soda → CO₂ → cake rises. Lemon juice on fish (acid + amine → ammonium salt → smell gone). Soil pH management: lime (CaO/Ca(OH)₂) neutralises acidic soil.',
    note: 'The pH of the resulting solution tells you whether the reaction is complete: pH 7 = full neutralisation (equal moles of strong acid and strong base). If there\'s excess acid, pH < 7; excess base, pH > 7. Buffer solutions resist pH change by absorbing added acid or base — blood uses a CO₂/bicarbonate buffer to hold pH at 7.4.'
  },
  fermentation: {
    title: 'Fermentation',
    tags: [{ label: 'Biochemical', col: '#27500A', bg: '#EAF3DE' }, { label: 'Anaerobic', col: '#712B13', bg: '#FAECE7' }],
    equation: 'C₆H₁₂O₆  →[yeast]  2 C₂H₅OH  +  2 CO₂',
    subtitle: '(glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide — yeast do this without oxygen)',
    what: 'Yeast convert glucose to ethanol and CO₂ in the absence of oxygen (anaerobic respiration). The glucose is partially broken down through glycolysis to pyruvate; instead of entering the Krebs cycle, it is converted to acetaldehyde (losing CO₂) and then to ethanol (gaining NADH). This releases only 2 ATP per glucose — vs ~30–32 in aerobic respiration. Yeast tolerate up to ~12–15% ethanol before it becomes toxic to them.',
    real: 'Beer, wine, bread (CO₂ from fermentation makes dough rise — the ethanol evaporates in baking), yogurt (lactic acid fermentation, not alcohol), vinegar (Acetobacter further oxidises ethanol). Also: biofuel ethanol, soy sauce, miso, and most traditional fermented foods worldwide.',
    note: 'Lactic acid fermentation (muscles, yogurt bacteria): glucose → lactic acid (no ethanol, no CO₂). This is what causes the burning sensation in muscles during intense exercise — and what sours milk into yogurt.'
  },
  polymerisation: {
    title: 'Polymerisation',
    tags: [{ label: 'Addition or Condensation', col: '#3C3489', bg: '#EEEDFE' }, { label: 'Chain-forming', col: '#444441', bg: '#F1EFE8' }],
    equation: 'n CH₂=CH₂  →  –[CH₂–CH₂]ₙ–   (addition polymerisation)\nn H₂N–R–COOH  →  –[NH–R–CO]ₙ–  +  n H₂O   (condensation polymerisation)',
    subtitle: '(many small monomers link into one giant polymer chain)',
    what: 'Addition polymerisation: monomers with C=C double bonds open up and link together. No atoms lost. Makes polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), PVC, and Teflon (PTFE). Condensation polymerisation: monomers react and lose a small molecule (usually H₂O) at each link. Makes polyester (–COO–), nylon (–CO–NH–), and proteins (amide bonds).',
    real: 'Polyethylene (carrier bags, bottles): –[CH₂–CH₂]ₙ– from ethylene gas. Nylon-6,6: diamine + diacid → polyamide (same amide bond as proteins). DNA: deoxyribonucleotide monomers linked by phosphodiester (ester-like) bonds. Your hair is a polyamide: keratin proteins linked by amide bonds and disulfide bridges (S–S, from cysteine residues).',
    note: 'Degree of polymerisation (n) typically ranges from 1,000 to 100,000+. Longer chains generally mean higher melting point, greater strength, and less flexibility. Ultra-high-molecular-weight polyethylene (UHMWPE, n ≈ 200,000) is used in bullet-proof vests and joint replacements.'
  }
};

function showRxn(key) {
  const r = reactions[key];
  setActiveChoice('#rxnBtnRow', key);

  let html = `<div style="font-size:13px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary); margin-bottom:8px;">${r.title}</div>`;
  html += `<div class="rxn-tags">`;
  r.tags.forEach(t => { html += `<span class="rxn-tag" style="background:${t.bg}; color:${t.col};">${t.label}</span>`; });
  html += `</div>`;
  html += `<div class="rxn-display" style="white-space:pre-line;">${r.equation}</div>`;
  html += `<div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom:10px; font-style:italic;">${r.subtitle}</div>`;
  html += `<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:8px; margin-bottom:8px;">`;
  html += `<div style="background:var(--color-background-secondary); border-radius:var(--border-radius-md); padding:9px 11px;"><div style="font-size:10px; color:var(--color-text-tertiary); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.06em; margin-bottom:4px;">What's happening</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-secondary); line-height:1.5;">${r.what}</div></div>`;
  html += `<div style="background:var(--color-background-secondary); border-radius:var(--border-radius-md); padding:9px 11px;"><div style="font-size:10px; color:var(--color-text-tertiary); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.06em; margin-bottom:4px;">Real-world examples</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-secondary); line-height:1.5;">${r.real}</div></div>`;
  html += `</div>`;
  html += `<div style="background:var(--color-background-secondary); border-radius:var(--border-radius-md); padding:9px 11px; font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-secondary); line-height:1.6;"><strong style="color:var(--color-text-primary); font-size:11px; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.05em;">Worth knowing · </strong>${r.note}</div>`;
  document.getElementById('rxn-display').innerHTML = html;
}
showRxn('combustion');

